## {{n-g|used in miscellaneous idiomatic phrases}}
##: {{uxi|fr|{{l|fr|pour '''de''' vrai}}|for real}}
##: {{uxi|fr|{{l|fr|pour '''de''' bon}}|for good}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Expresses possession, origin, source, material, contents, cause, manner, or means, and can correspond to English [[of]], [['s]], [[from]], [[about]], or other prepositions depending on context.
* When {{m|fr|de}} is followed by a definite article, the two combine to give the following combined forms:
    {| class="wikitable"
    ! de + article
    ! Combined form
    |-
    | align="center" | de + {{l|fr|le}}
    | align="center" | {{l|fr|du}}
    |-
    | align="center" | {{l|fr|de la|de + la}}
    | align="center" | {{small|''unchanged'' <br>({{l|fr|de la}})}}
    |-
    | align="center" | de + {{l|fr|l'}}
    | align="center" | {{small|''unchanged'' <br>(de l')}}
    |-
    | align="center" | de + {{l|fr|les}}
    | align="center" | {{l|fr|des}}
    |}
:* Before a word beginning with a vowel sound, {{m|fr|de}} elides to {{m|fr|d'|d’}}.
:* The contractions {{m|fr|du}} and {{m|fr|des}} occur only when {{m|fr|de}} is followed by the definite articles {{m|fr|le}} and {{m|fr|les}}. They do not occur when {{m|fr|le}}, {{m|fr|la}}, or {{m|fr|les}} are object pronouns.
:: {{uxi|fr|J'essaie '''de le''' convaincre.|I'm trying to convince him.}}
* Logically following the previously described rule, {{m|fr|de}} combines with the relative pronouns, which themselves contain an element of the definite article, producing these forms:
    {| class="wikitable"
    ! de + article
    ! Combined form
    |-
    | align="center" | de + {{l|fr|lequel}}
    | align="center" | {{l|fr|duquel}}
    |-
    | align="center" | de + {{l|fr|laquelle}}
    | align="center" | {{m|fr||de laquelle}}
    |-
    | align="center" | de + {{l|fr|lesquels}}
    | align="center" | {{l|fr|desquels}}
    |-
    | align="center" | de + {{l|fr|lesquelles}}
    | align="center" | {{l|fr|desquelles}}
    |}
* Certain French verbs require {{m|fr|de}} after them, either as part of a fixed construction or to distinguish a particular meaning or usage. In many cases, English translations do not use a corresponding preposition. Some {{m|fr|de}} + infinitive constructions are fixed expressions selected by particular verbs, nouns, or adjectives, such as {{m|fr|avoir besoin de|t=to need to}} and {{m|fr|avoir envie|avoir envie de|t=to want to}}.
* {{m|fr|de|De}} is commonly used after expressions of quantity: beaucoup de, trop de, assez de, peu de, etc.

