===Etymology 2===
{{PIE word|nb|yúHs}}
{{inh+|nb|gmq-mno|-}} ''ther'', from {{inh|nb|non|þér|t=you, plural}}, a variant of {{m|non|ér}} with initial ''þ-'' generalized from preceding verb forms ending in ''-ið'', from {{inh|nb|gem-pro|*jīz}}, a Northwest Germanic variant of {{inh|nb|gem-pro|*jūz}}, from {{inh|nb|ine-pro|*yúHs|t=you, plural}}.

In many Norwegian dialects, the corresponding pronoun instead continues Middle Norwegian ''thit'', from {{m|non|þit|t=you two}}, a variant of {{m|non|it}}, from Proto-Germanic {{m|gem-pro|*jut}}. The old dual and plural forms merged as the dual distinction disappeared.<ref>Eric Papazian (2008), “[https://web.novus.no/MOM/MOM_2008-1.pdf De – dykk – dere. Andre person flertall i nordisk, særlig norsk]”, ''Maal og Minne'', no. 1, pages 70–71, 76–79.</ref>

Cognate with {{cog|nn|de}}, {{cog|da|I}}, {{cog|sv|ni}}, {{cog|is|þér}}, {{cog|de|ihr|t=you, plural}} and {{cog|en|ye}}.

